"texte": [
"Every few months, international polling agencies release barometers of public trust whose findings read like a slow-motion seismograph of democratic malaise. Across established democracies, confidence in parliaments, political parties, mainstream news organizations, trade unions, and even judicial and scientific bodies has experienced a steady, multi-decade erosion. As a columnist tracking institutional politics, I am struck by the fact that this crisis rarely announces itself through dramatic revolutionary upheavals; instead, it operates as a silent hollowing-out of legitimacy, leaving public institutions standing like imposing architectural facades whose inner authority has quietly evaporated.",
"Several structural forces converge to explain this widespread institutional disaffection. First, repeated failures of accountability—from financial bailouts that socialized private banking losses to unfulfilled political promises and corruption scandals—have convinced many citizens that traditional institutions serve entrenched elites rather than the common good. Second, the sluggish bureaucratic tempo of representative bodies appears increasingly out of step with an era of instantaneous digital expectations, fostering frustration at governments' inability to resolve complex crises such as housing affordability or climate disruption.",
"Compounding this frustration is the disruptive impact of digital media ecosystems. By bypassing traditional editorial gatekeepers, social platforms have democratized public expression, yet their engagement-driven algorithms systematically reward outrage, sensationalism, and conspiratorial distrust over nuanced institutional deliberation. In such an environment, authority itself—whether grounded in democratic mandate, legal expertise, or scientific peer review—is routinely caricatured as elitist manipulation.",
"It would be profoundly misguided, however, to celebrate the collapse of traditional institutions as a triumph of grassroots freedom. Institutions are the invisible scaffolding of civilized life: they mediate social conflicts peacefully, protect vulnerable minorities against the tyranny of fleeting majorities, and preserve collective memory across generations.",
"Ultimately, overcoming this silent crisis requires neither blind deference to outdated hierarchies nor nihilistic anti-institutionalism. Traditional institutions can regain public trust only by radically reforming themselves—embracing genuine transparency, opening decision-making processes to citizen participation, and demonstrating through tangible social justice that they exist to serve the dignity of every citizen."
],
