    "texte": [
      "At dusk, as millions of illuminated windows flicker to life across the towering glass facades of the metropolis, the city presents a mesmerizing spectacle of human density and collective energy. Down on the rain-slicked avenues, commuters brush shoulders in crowded subway carriages and hurry past bustling cafés, surrounded at every turn by thousands of strangers. Yet behind thousands of those uniform apartment doors, a quiet, pervasive epidemic unfolds in silence: chronic urban loneliness. This poignant paradox—feeling profoundly isolated precisely where human proximity is greatest—has emerged as one of the defining psychosocial challenges of contemporary metropolitan life.",
      "As the urban sociologist Georg Simmel observed over a century ago in his seminal essay 'The Metropolis and Mental Life,' city dwellers develop a protective 'blasé attitude'—a psychological shield of polite indifference—to avoid being overwhelmed by the relentless sensory and social stimulation of urban crowds. While this emotional detachment preserves mental equilibrium amidst anonymity, contemporary urban design and economic pressures have pushed it to an extreme. Rising housing costs have fueled a dramatic surge in single-person households, while the privatization of public squares, the decline of neighborhood shops, and the dominance of automobile-centric infrastructure have eroded the casual, unscripted encounters that once knitted urban neighborhoods together.",
