       "JOURNALIST: Beyond the digital divide, how does geography shape these contemporary forms of marginalization?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Spatial relegation has become a decisive engine of inequality. Driven by soaring metropolitan real estate prices, working-class and lower-middle-class families are increasingly pushed out to peripheral suburbs or rural hinterlands suffering from the withdrawal of public transport, post offices, maternity wards, and quality schools. Living in these 'institutional deserts' compounds economic hardship with geographic immobility, trapping residents far from dynamic job markets and cultural opportunities.",
