"texte": [
"Dear Clara, as I sit on the balcony watching the evening ferry cross the harbor, I find myself reflecting on our brief video call yesterday—and on how easily three months had slipped by since our last real conversation despite the dozens of voice notes and emojis we exchanged in between. Living in different cities while working in digital ethics, I am acutely grateful for the technologies that keep our friendship alive across borders. Yet I cannot shake a lingering unease about how subtly digital mediation has altered the texture and resilience of human bonds in our generation.",
"As the psychologist Sherry Turkle insightfully observed in her exploration of connected solitude, digital communication promises us three seductive illusions: that we can put our attention wherever we want, that we will always be heard, and that we will never have to be alone. In practice, however, replacing unhurried physical presence with asynchronous messaging often yields what sociologists call 'thin ties'—connections characterized by constant superficial contact but diminished emotional depth and mutual vulnerability.",
"True friendship and civic trust require friction, patience, and shared presence in real time. When we communicate predominantly through edited texts or curated social feeds, we unconsciously filter out awkward silences, spontaneous vulnerability, and the subtle bodily cues through which genuine empathy is forged. Worse still, the ease with which digital connections can be muted, ghosted, or severed at the slightest discomfort fosters a disposable attitude toward relationships, eroding our capacity for difficult reconciliation.",
