"Excellencies, distinguished delegates, and members of the Global Economic Assembly, we convene at a moment of striking historical paradox. Over the past four decades, global economic integration, scientific breakthroughs, and digital connectivity have generated an unprecedented expansion of aggregate human wealth. Yet alongside this rising tide of global output, economic inequality within nations—and between the ultra-wealthy elite and the bottom half of humanity—has reached levels unseen since the Gilded Age of the late nineteenth century. Today, the richest one percent of the global population captures nearly twice as much new wealth as the rest of the world combined, while billions of working citizens struggle with stagnant real wages and eroding social mobility.",
"This extreme concentration of wealth is neither an accidental byproduct of market efficiency nor an immutable law of nature; it is the direct consequence of deliberate institutional and fiscal choices. As economist Thomas Piketty demonstrated in his landmark historical research, whenever the rate of return on capital consistently exceeds the rate of overall economic growth, inherited and financial wealth automatically compounds faster than income earned through labor. Compounding this structural dynamic is a globalized tax architecture riddled with offshore havens and regulatory arbitrage, allowing multinational corporations and billionaires to shift profits across jurisdictions and pay lower effective tax rates than nurses, teachers, or factory workers.",
"Furthermore, in an interconnected world, extreme economic inequality ceases to be a purely moral concern and becomes a systemic threat to democratic stability and ecological survival. Concentrated economic power inevitably translates into disproportionate political influence through campaign financing and media ownership, hollowing out equal citizenship. Simultaneously, the luxury consumption and carbon-intensive investments of the global top percentile generate vastly disproportionate greenhouse gas emissions, while low-income communities bear the brunt of climate disasters.",
"Some defenders of laissez-faire economics still invoke the 'trickle-down' hypothesis, claiming that tax cuts and deregulation for top earners stimulate investment that eventually benefits everyone. Decades of empirical evidence, however, have thoroughly discredited this assumption: excessive inequality actually dampens aggregate consumer demand, starves public education and infrastructure of vital funding, and stifles human potential.",
"Therefore, delegates, confronting economic inequality in the twenty-first century requires coordinated international action matching the global scale of capital itself. Establishing a binding global minimum tax on multinational corporations and extreme wealth, closing tax havens, and reinvesting revenues into universal public education, healthcare, and green infrastructure represent the essential foundations of a stable, dignified, and genuinely shared global economy."
],
