"texte": [
"Excellencies, Ministers of Finance, and distinguished delegates gathered for this Summit on Global Development, over sixty years after the wave of political decolonization reshaped the map of the world, a fundamental truth remains self-evident: constitutional independence remains fragile so long as a nation lacks genuine economic sovereignty. Today, dozens of developing countries across Africa, Latin America, and Asia find their policy space severely constrained by inherited structural dependencies—locked into exporting unprocessed raw commodities, reliant on volatile foreign capital inflows, and burdened by external debts denominated in foreign currencies over which they exercise zero monetary control.",
"This structural vulnerability is compounded by an international financial architecture designed in 1944 that no longer reflects global demographic or economic reality. Developing nations routinely pay borrowing interest rates three to four times higher than wealthy economies due to biased risk assessments by private credit rating agencies, trapping governments in a vicious cycle where debt servicing consumes more public revenue than healthcare and education combined. Simultaneously, bilateral investment treaties and intellectual property rules frequently restrict developing states from nurturing domestic manufacturing or accessing green technologies on affordable terms.",
"True economic sovereignty in the twenty-first century, however, does not mean retreating into isolationist autarky—a strategy that has historically bred technological stagnation and scarcity. Rather, it means possessing the democratic autonomy to formulate national industrial, agricultural, and monetary strategies aligned with domestic human development while engaging with the global economy on equitable terms.",
"Achieving this autonomy requires bold action on two fronts. Domestically, developing nations must mobilize local savings, broaden progressive tax bases, invest heavily in technical education and regional value chains, and add industrial value to their own minerals and agricultural products prior to export. Internationally, the Global South must speak with a unified voice to demand deep reform of multilateral development banks, sovereign debt restructuring mechanisms, and fair technology transfers.",
"Delegates, economic sovereignty is not a nostalgic slogan; it is the indispensable foundation of human dignity. Only when developing nations command the fiscal and industrial tools to shape their own destiny can global interdependence evolve from an asymmetric hierarchy into a genuine partnership of equals."
],
