"JOURNALIST: Dr. Mensah, according to the International Labour Organization, more than two billion people worldwide—representing over sixty percent of the global workforce—earn their livelihoods in the informal economy. Why does this vast sphere of unregistered economic activity persist so stubbornly across developing and emerging economies?\nDR. MENSAH: Because the informal economy is not a marginal anomaly; it is the primary survival engine for hundreds of millions of households excluded from formal salaried employment. From street vendors and smallholder farmers to artisanal mechanics and home-based garment workers, informal enterprise flourishes wherever formal job creation lags behind rapid demographic growth, or where burdensome bureaucratic registration costs and complex tax codes price small entrepreneurs out of legality.",
"JOURNALIST: Many policymakers view the informal sector purely as an obstacle to modernization that deprives states of tax revenue. Is that assessment entirely fair?\nDR. MENSAH: It captures only half the picture, overlooking a profound socio-economic contradiction. On one hand, informal markets demonstrate extraordinary entrepreneurial agility, supply affordable essential goods and food to low-income urban neighborhoods, and act as an indispensable shock absorber during economic downturns when formal factories lay off workers. Without the informal sector, many rapidly growing cities would face immediate social collapse.",
"JOURNALIST: What, then, is the darker side of this informality that makes it a trap for both workers and national development?\nDR. MENSAH: The human cost for workers is severe. Operating outside labor regulations means zero access to health insurance, paid maternity leave, workplace safety standards, or old-age pensions—leaving families one medical emergency away from destitution. Simultaneously, at the macroeconomic level, informal micro-enterprises lack legal property titles and credit histories, preventing them from securing bank loans to invest in machinery and scale up productivity, while the resulting narrow tax base starves governments of funds for schools, roads, and hospitals.",
