"texte": [
"Madam President, esteemed fellows of the Academy of Sciences, and young researchers embarking upon your scientific vocations, we gather in an age when human ingenuity has unlocked powers that earlier civilizations attributed solely to myth. From splitting the atom and editing the human genome to engineering autonomous weapons and planetary-scale climate interventions, modern science has vastly expanded the reach of human action. Yet this extraordinary expansion of technical capability compels us to confront an enduring ethical truth: the fact that science can accomplish something does not, by itself, establish that humanity ought to do it.",
"For centuries, scientific modernity drew inspiration from Francis Bacon's vision of knowledge as power over nature, coupled with the belief that pure scientific inquiry is morally neutral—leaving ethical responsibility solely to politicians or citizens who apply discoveries later. However, as the philosopher Hans Jonas argued in his landmark work 'The Imperative of Responsibility,' the sheer scale and irreversibility of modern technological power have rendered that traditional separation obsolete. When a single laboratory breakthrough or engineering deployment can alter the biosphere or human genetics for millennia, moral reflection can no longer arrive as an afterthought.",
"History offers sobering reminders of the price paid when scientific brilliance outpaces ethical restraint. From the development of thermonuclear arsenals capable of obliterating civilization to the uncritical deployment of synthetic chemicals and fossil-fuel technologies that destabilized Earth's climate, human ingenuity untempered by foresight has repeatedly generated existential hazards.",
"Recognizing moral boundaries does not mean stifling legitimate curiosity or censoring inconvenient empirical truths; on the contrary, scientific integrity demands fearless honesty about evidence. Rather, moral responsibility requires embedding precautionary ethics, interdisciplinary dialogue, and democratic accountability directly into the governance of research—especially in high-risk domains such as dual-use pathogens, autonomous warfare, and artificial general intelligence.",
"Therefore, fellows and young scholars, let us honor the noblest tradition of science not merely through technical mastery, but through ethical wisdom. True scientific greatness in the twenty-first century lies in dedicating our discoveries to the preservation of life, human dignity, and a habitable planet for all generations yet to come."
