"JOURNALIST: Professor Vance, in an age of smartphones, biometric cameras, and predictive analytics, privacy is sometimes dismissed as an outdated luxury—or met with the familiar refrain, 'If you have nothing to hide, you have nothing to fear.' As a scholar of digital constitutional law, why do you argue that privacy remains indispensable to democratic freedom?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Because the 'nothing to hide' cliché fundamentally misunderstands both power and human autonomy. Privacy is not about concealing shameful secrets; it is the protected personal sanctuary within which individuals think freely, experiment with unconventional ideas, form intimate relationships, and organize political dissent without fear of scrutiny or retaliation. When every search query, location trace, and biometric expression is continuously logged, citizens internalize an invisible gaze—producing a 'chilling effect' that encourages timid self-censorship and conformity.",
"JOURNALIST: Public debate often focuses on state surveillance by intelligence agencies, yet you emphasize that commercial data harvesting is equally consequential. How do corporate and governmental surveillance intersect today?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: We now inhabit what scholar Shoshana Zuboff aptly termed 'surveillance capitalism.' Tech conglomerates extract granular behavioral data from billions of users to construct detailed psychological profiles used for targeted advertising and behavioral manipulation. Crucially, this vast commercial data infrastructure rarely remains sealed from state power: intelligence agencies and law enforcement routinely purchase data from commercial brokers or compel platform disclosures, creating a seamless public-private surveillance apparatus.",
"JOURNALIST: Governments frequently justify expanded biometric facial recognition and predictive policing by citing public safety, counterterrorism, and crime prevention. How should democracies balance security imperatives against fundamental privacy rights?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Framing security and privacy as zero-sum enemies is a dangerous false dichotomy. Mass, indiscriminate surveillance rarely prevents sophisticated threats—in fact, drowning analysts in oceans of irrelevant data often obscures genuine warnings—while disproportionately targeting racial and socioeconomic minorities through biased facial-recognition algorithms. Democratic security requires targeted, judicially warranted investigation based on individualized suspicion, never blanket suspicionless monitoring of entire populations.",
"JOURNALIST: What legal and technological safeguards are urgently required to restore meaningful privacy in the digital age?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: First, legislatures must ban real-time biometric mass surveillance in public spaces and outlaw the commercial sale of personal data by opaque data brokers. Second, we must enforce strict data minimization—requiring companies to collect only data strictly necessary for a service—and protect end-to-end encryption as a cornerstone of civic liberty. Privacy is not an individual whim; it is the collective oxygen of any free society."
],
