    "texte": [
      "Half a century after the Apollo moon landings symbolized the ideological rivalry of the Cold War, humanity has entered a radically different 'New Space Age.' Driven by reusable launch vehicles, plummeting orbital payload costs, and ambitious national lunar and Martian programs, outer space is no longer the exclusive domain of two superpowers engaged in symbolic prestige races. Today, dozens of emerging spacefaring nations alongside powerful private aerospace billionaires are competing to deploy mega-constellations of communications satellites, establish permanent lunar bases, and prospect for rare-earth minerals and water ice on asteroids and the Moon.",
       "Behind this scientific and commercial renaissance, however, lies an intensifying geopolitical contest with profound implications for terrestrial security. Modern civilian and military infrastructures—from global positioning navigation and financial timing networks to climate monitoring and missile early-warning systems—depend critically on vulnerable orbital satellites. As major powers establish dedicated military space commands and test anti-satellite weapons capable of blinding an adversary's communications in minutes, low Earth orbit risks becoming a perilous theater of asymmetric warfare.",
"Simultaneously, the rapid commercialization of space has outpaced the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, which declared celestial bodies the 'province of all mankind' and prohibited national territorial sovereignty in space. Ambiguities surrounding private resource extraction on the Moon, the monopolization of prime orbital slots by private satellite mega-constellations, and the escalating accumulation of hazardous space debris threaten to trigger a 'tragedy of the commons' beyond Earth's atmosphere.",
