"texte": [
"During a recent national election campaign, a hyper-realistic synthetic audio recording purporting to capture a leading candidate conspiring to rig ballot boxes went viral across messaging apps within forty-eight hours—garnering millions of shares before forensic audio analysts could expose it as an artificial intelligence fabrication. Covering information warfare as a media columnist over the past decade, I have watched digital disinformation evolve from clumsy email hoaxes into an industrial-scale epistemological threat capable of poisoning democratic deliberation and public health.",
"Unlike innocent 'misinformation'—falsehoods shared inadvertently without malicious intent—disinformation involves the deliberate, coordinated creation and amplification of deceptive narratives for political, financial, or geopolitical gain. Digital technologies have supercharged this age-old tactic in two decisive ways: generative AI has collapsed the cost of producing convincing deepfake videos, cloned voices, and synthetic news articles, while engagement-maximizing social media algorithms systematically prioritize emotionally charged falsehoods, which studies show spread six times faster than sober factual corrections.",
