    "texte": [
      "JOURNALIST: Professor Vance, since the landmark 1987 Brundtland Report defined sustainable development as meeting present needs without compromising future generations—and since the adoption of the UN Sustainable Development Goals in 2015—'sustainability' has become ubiquitous in corporate boardrooms and government summits. Yet global emissions and biodiversity loss continue to climb. Why does such a wide chasm persist between lofty rhetoric and tangible reality?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Because the concept of sustainable development has been systematically diluted into a comforting slogan that avoids confronting hard structural trade-offs. Many corporations and governments treat sustainability as a public-relations veneer—engaging in 'greenwashing' through vague net-zero pledges set decades into the future while continuing to subsidize fossil fuels, promote planned obsolescence, and measure national success exclusively by short-term GDP expansion.",
      "JOURNALIST: Critics from the Global South also argue that international sustainability frameworks often overlook historical inequities. How valid is that critique?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: It is profoundly valid. Wealthy industrialized nations built their prosperity over two centuries by burning cheap fossil fuels and depleting global ecological space, yet they frequently lecture developing countries on conservation without delivering promised climate finance or affordable green technology transfers. True sustainability cannot be achieved through ecological austerity imposed upon the poor while the affluent maintain hyper-consumptive lifestyles.",
"JOURNALIST: Does this gap mean the very idea of sustainable development is fundamentally flawed, or can it still serve as a transformative compass?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: The flaw lies not in the core vision, but in treating economic, social, and environmental goals as three separate, equal circles where financial profit always takes precedence in practice. Economist Kate Raworth's 'Doughnut Economics' model offers a far more rigorous framework: humanity must ensure a social foundation of dignity, healthcare, education, and housing for every person while staying strictly within the ecological ceiling of Earth's planetary boundaries.",
"JOURNALIST: What concrete institutional shifts would signal that societies are finally moving from symbolic green pledges to genuine sustainability?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Three decisive steps: outlawing unverified corporate greenwashing claims, redirecting trillions in environmentally harmful subsidies toward public transit, agroecology, and renewable grids, and embedding legally binding ecological budgets alongside financial budgets in national parliaments. Sustainability becomes real only when planetary limits carry the same legal force as fiscal solvency."
],
