    "texte": [
      "During a strategic symposium on international relations in Vienna, diplomatic historian Prof. Elena Rostova and geopolitical strategist Dr. Marcus Vance debated the contours of the emerging world order. 'The brief post-Cold War "unipolar moment," during which a single superpower underwrote global security and liberal economic norms, has definitively ended,' Prof. Rostova observed. 'Driven by the economic ascent of Asia, the growing diplomatic assertiveness of middle powers across the Global South, and renewed great-power competition, international politics has entered an era of complex multipolarity.'",
      "Dr. Vance nodded thoughtfully while warning against romanticizing a multipolar balance of power: 'History teaches us that transitions from hegemonic order to multipolarity are inherently perilous, Elena. Look at Europe prior to 1914 or the 1930s: when multiple rival power centers compete over spheres of influence, critical minerals, maritime chokepoints, and technological supremacy without universally accepted rules, miscalculation and proxy conflicts escalate rapidly.'",
"'Historical analogies with 1914 are instructive as warnings, yet they overlook crucial structural differences today,' Prof. Rostova replied. 'Unlike rigid nineteenth-century imperial alliances, contemporary middle powers—from Brazil, India, and Indonesia to South Africa and Gulf states—are practicing "multi-alignment." Rather than choosing exclusive ideological camps, they maintain pragmatic trade, technological, and security ties with multiple major powers simultaneously, creating cross-cutting interdependencies that can actually restrain reckless escalation.'",
