"texte": [
"Observe passengers in any airport lounge or metropolitan subway car today, and you will witness a quiet revolution in human attention: whereas printed books and long-form essays once filled idle hours, nearly every pair of eyes is now fixed upon glowing smartphone screens, skimming rapid bursts of short-form videos, headlines, and social updates. As a literary critic and cultural columnist tracking reading habits over the past twenty years, I am struck by a subtle paradox: modern citizens read more words per day via text messages and online feeds than any previous generation, yet sustained, immersive 'deep reading' of complex books is steadily declining.",
"As cognitive neuroscientist Maryanne Wolf has demonstrated in her research on the reading brain, reading is not an innate biological instinct like spoken language; it is a demanding cultural invention that rewires neural circuits for sustained focus, inferential reasoning, analogical thinking, and empathetic perspective-taking. When our daily reading diet shifts overwhelmingly toward rapid digital skimming—jumping impatiently across hyperlinks and notifications—the brain adapts to speed and novelty at the expense of contemplative depth.",
"This erosion of deep reading carries profound intellectual and democratic consequences. Complex novels, historical works, and philosophical essays train the mind to inhabit unfamiliar viewpoints patiently, follow intricate multi-step arguments, and tolerate ambiguity—capacities essential for resisting simplistic demagoguery in civic life.",
"Of course, digital formats have also democratized access to literature through e-books, audiobooks, and open online libraries for millions of readers worldwide; the adversary is not digital technology itself, but commercial platforms engineered to fracture sustained attention.",
"Reclaiming the transformative power of reading therefore requires deliberate cultural resistance: protecting quiet offline sanctuaries for uninterrupted books, revitalizing public libraries and school reading programs, and recognizing deep reading not as an elitist pastime, but as an indispensable practice of intellectual freedom."
],
