    "texte": [
      "Half a century ago, cultural life across industrial societies was anchored in shared physical rituals and synchronized mass media: families gathered around a single living-room television broadcast, neighborhoods queued outside local cinemas on Friday evenings, and municipal concert halls or public libraries served as common civic meeting grounds. Observing cultural shifts as an arts and society columnist over the past two decades, I have watched this cohesive cultural landscape transform into an endlessly personalized, on-demand digital universe where two siblings living under the same roof may inhabit entirely separate artistic worlds.",
      "This generational metamorphosis reflects both technological disruption and changing social values. Whereas older generations often navigated a clear distinction between consecrated 'high culture'—symphonic music, canonical literature, museum retrospectives—and commercial popular entertainment, younger cohorts increasingly reject rigid cultural hierarchies. Raised within interactive digital ecosystems, Gen Z and millennials favor 'participatory culture': remixing music tracks, producing short-form visual narratives, engaging in collaborative gaming universes, and blurring the traditional boundary between passive audience and active creator.",
