"texte": [
"Provost, Deans of Science and Letters, esteemed faculty, and incoming undergraduates, across universities worldwide today, funding priorities, political speeches, and parental expectations overwhelmingly converge upon a single acronym: STEM—science, technology, engineering, and mathematics. In an era defined by artificial intelligence, biotechnology, and clean-energy engineering, technical fluency is undeniably vital. Yet as humanities departments face budget contractions and skeptical questions regarding their immediate 'return on investment,' I stand before you to affirm an urgent truth: the more technologically powerful our civilization becomes, the more indispensable the humanities grow for human survival and wisdom.",
"Technology excels at answering questions of 'how'—how to sequence a genome, optimize a supply chain, or train a neural network—yet technical instruments alone remain silent on questions of 'why' and 'ought.' Literature, philosophy, history, ethics, and the arts are not decorative luxuries reserved for leisure hours; they are the rigorous intellectual laboratories in which humanity examines moral responsibility, interprets cultural memory, interrogates power, and imagines what constitutes a dignified human life.",
"Moreover, treating the humanities and the sciences as rival, mutually exclusive cultures is a profoundly damaging modern fallacy. Complex twenty-first-century challenges—from designing ethical artificial intelligence and governing climate transitions to navigating public health crises—cannot be solved by coders or engineers working in isolation. They require leaders who unite quantitative literacy with historical perspective, ethical discernment, narrative empathy, and cross-cultural understanding.",
"As philosopher Martha Nussbaum warned in her defense of liberal education, societies that reduce higher education solely to short-term technical utility risk producing 'useful profit-makers' stripped of the critical imagination and empathetic citizenship required to sustain healthy democracies.",
"Therefore, incoming students, whether you major in computer science or comparative literature, refuse any narrow intellectual compartmentalization. Forge a bold alliance between scientific ingenuity and humanistic wisdom, so that the technologies you build and govern serve the highest aspirations of human dignity."
],
