"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Lindholm, when the World Wide Web was invented three decades ago, pioneers envisioned a universal digital library granting every human being on Earth free access to humanity's accumulated scientific and cultural knowledge. Thirty years on, how close have we come to realizing that Enlightenment ideal?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: We stand suspended between extraordinary progress and stubborn new enclosures. On one hand, initiatives like Wikipedia, open-source software, massive open online courses, and public digital repositories have unlocked unprecedented access to educational resources for millions of learners worldwide. On the other hand, vast swathes of peer-reviewed scientific research—ironically funded by public taxpayers—remain locked behind exorbitant commercial journal paywalls costing thousands of dollars per subscription, effectively excluding researchers, physicians, and students across the Global South and underfunded institutions.",
"JOURNALIST: Beyond academic paywalls, what structural inequalities prevent millions of citizens from benefiting from online knowledge even when content is nominally free?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: Access to raw data does not automatically translate into genuine knowledge empowerment. Nearly one-third of humanity still lacks reliable, affordable broadband connectivity or consistent electricity. Furthermore, over sixty percent of web content is published in English, marginalizing speakers of thousands of non-dominant languages, while unequal access to critical digital literacy leaves disadvantaged learners vulnerable to algorithmic misinformation rather than verified scholarship.",
"JOURNALIST: Now, generative artificial intelligence models trained on the open web are introducing a new tension around intellectual property and knowledge commons. How should societies navigate this shift?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: It is a profound legal and ethical crossroads. If commercial AI corporations scrape decades of volunteer-created public knowledge commons and copyrighted journalism to build proprietary, closed-source models behind expensive subscriptions, we risk witnessing a massive private enclosure of humanity's shared intellectual heritage. Conversely, transparent, publicly governed open-science AI tools could democratize translation, tutoring, and medical research globally.",
"JOURNALIST: What concrete policies are needed to transform universal access to knowledge from an aspirational slogan into a lived global reality?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: First, governments and research funders must mandate immediate open-access publication for all publicly funded science. Second, we must invest in universal broadband infrastructure, multilingual educational repositories, and public digital libraries. Knowledge is unique among human resources: sharing it never diminishes its supply, and treating it as a global public good is the surest path to shared human progress."
