"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Vance, most people sincerely believe their daily judgments—whether hiring a job applicant, evaluating a student's essay, or assessing a patient's symptoms—are guided by objective merit and rational evidence. How has contemporary social psychology challenged that comforting self-image?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Profoundly. Decades of experimental research demonstrate that human decision-making is routinely shaped by 'implicit bias'—unconscious mental associations and stereotypes absorbed automatically from our cultural environment. Unlike overt, conscious bigotry that an individual openly endorses, implicit prejudices operate beneath conscious awareness, subtly distorting how we interpret ambiguous behavior even among people who genuinely champion fairness and equality.",
"JOURNALIST: Could you illustrate how these unconscious biases manifest in high-stakes real-world institutions?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Field audit studies provide striking proof. When researchers submit identical resumes differing only in whether the applicant's name sounds traditionally male or female, or suggests an ethnic minority background, callback rates diverge dramatically. Similar patterns appear in judicial sentencing disparities, bank loan approvals, and medical pain management, where unconscious assumptions lead decision-makers to scrutinize marginalized individuals more harshly while granting members of dominant groups the 'benefit of the doubt.'",
