"texte": [
"Inside the wood-paneled municipal council chamber, twelve board members raised their hands in swift, practiced unanimity to approve a lucrative coastal resort contract that bypassed environmental impact hearings. Seated near the end of the table, urban planner Elena Vance felt her pulse quicken as the chairman's gaze swept expectantly toward her for the final affirmative vote. Raising her hand would preserve collegial warmth, career advancement, and peaceful anonymity; lowering it meant inviting frostiness, mockery, and professional isolation. In that quiet, suspended second, Elena experienced firsthand what political thinkers have long recognized: the sharpest threat to freedom of thought in democratic societies rarely arrives as state censorship, but as the seductive comfort of social conformity.",
"As John Stuart Mill warned in his classic essay 'On Liberty,' society can exercise a 'social tyranny' more pervasive than many forms of political oppression—penetrating deeply into daily life and enslaving the soul itself through subtle ridicule, ostracism, and moral orthodoxy. Classic social psychology experiments, notably Solomon Asch's conformity studies, confirmed how powerfully human beings distrust their own eyes rather than endure the discomfort of standing alone against a unanimous group.",
"In the digital age, this ancient tribal instinct is amplified exponentially by algorithmic echo chambers and viral public shaming: expressing a nuanced or dissenting view online can trigger instantaneous reputational exile, incentivizing cautious self-censorship.",
