    "texte": [
      "At an interdisciplinary symposium on cognitive science in Zurich, decision philosopher Prof. Julian Mercer and affective neuroscientist Dr. Elena Rostova debated the classical opposition between reason and emotion. 'From Plato's metaphor of the charioteer reining in unruly passions to Enlightenment rationalism, Western thought has long treated emotions as dangerous cognitive pollutants that cloud objective judgment,' Prof. Mercer observed. 'In public policy, judicial sentencing, and financial markets, impulsive fear, anger, or tribal pride routinely distort risk assessment and provoke catastrophic miscalculations.'",
      "Dr. Rostova nodded appreciatively before presenting groundbreaking neurological evidence that complicates that traditional dualism: 'Unchecked emotional reactivity certainly distorts judgment, Julian, yet modern affective neuroscience has dismantled the myth that pure reason can function without emotion. In his famous clinical studies of patients with damage to the ventromedial prefrontal cortex—individuals whose logical IQ remained completely intact while their emotional signaling was severed—neuroscientist Antonio Damasio discovered that they became utterly paralyzed by even trivial daily decisions.'",
      "'That clinical finding is fascinating,' Prof. Mercer replied. 'How precisely do emotional signals assist rational deliberation rather than derailing it?'",
