"texte": [
"Distinguished guests, human rights advocates, and fellow citizens gathered for this Civic Conscience Assembly, whenever large-scale injustices unfold—whether systemic racial or gender discrimination, environmental devastation, or authoritarian abuses—a comforting psychological alibi inevitably surfaces among ordinary bystanders: 'I did not design this system, I am merely one individual among millions, and nothing I alone do can alter the course of history.' Yet the entire moral history of humanity testifies against this fatalistic rationalization. Collective injustices rarely endure solely through the malice of active perpetrators; they survive through the quiet compliance, routine silence, and comfortable indifference of millions who tell themselves they are powerless.",
"As the political theorist Hannah Arendt demonstrated in her profound reflections on 'the banality of evil' and moral responsibility under unjust regimes, the most dangerous abdication of human dignity occurs when individuals stop thinking independently and hide behind institutional obedience or statistical insignificance. In a deeply interconnected society, neutrality in the face of systemic oppression is never morally innocent; as Desmond Tutu famously observed, choosing neutrality in situations of injustice amounts to choosing the side of the oppressor.",
