"texte": [
"In contemporary meritocratic societies, visible markers of success—prestigious university credentials, lucrative executive careers, social media influence, and material affluence—are celebrated as the ultimate validation of human worth. Yet beneath the glossy surface of achievement culture lies a striking psychosocial paradox: many individuals who reach the pinnacle of conventional success report profound inner emptiness, chronic anxiety, and alienation, while the relentless race for status corrodes social solidarity across society as a whole.",
"As political philosopher Michael Sandel demonstrated in 'The Tyranny of Merit,' modern meritocratic ideology rests on a seductive but flawed moral premise: that those who succeed financially owe their triumph entirely to their own talent and grit, while those who struggle have only themselves to blame. This worldview inflicts a double psychological injury: it breeds arrogant hubris among winners—who overlook the roles of inherited privilege, luck, and public infrastructure in their success—while inflicting toxic humiliation upon working-class citizens whose vital contributions to society are undervalued.",
"Simultaneously, equating human dignity exclusively with competitive economic triumph traps even high achievers on an endless 'hedonic treadmill.' Because external status is inherently positional—meaning one's prestige depends on outranking others—every milestone achieved swiftly loses its luster, demanding ever greater sacrifices of health, leisure, and intimate relationships.",
"Redefining success does not mean disparaging excellence, disciplined effort, or legitimate professional ambition; rather, it requires liberating human worth from narrow market valuation.",
"Ultimately, a mature civilization measures success not by the accumulation of solitary trophies, but by the quality of human relationships, the dignity accorded to all forms of socially useful labor, and the generosity with which individuals contribute to the common good."
