    "texte": [
      "Although the 1946 Constitution of the World Health Organization proclaimed the highest attainable standard of health as a fundamental right of every human being, a child's life expectancy today remains dictated to a staggering degree by an accident of birth. A newborn in a high-income nation can expect to live past eighty years with immediate access to precision oncology, neonatal intensive care, and essential pharmaceuticals, whereas a child born in an impoverished rural district of the Global South faces a life expectancy nearly twenty-five years shorter and starkly elevated risks of preventable maternal and infectious mortality.",
      "Crucially, these global health disparities are neither natural medical inevitabilities nor merely the result of local clinical shortages; they are deeply rooted in structural economic and geopolitical asymmetries. While low-income countries bear over eighty percent of the global burden of preventable disease, they command less than two percent of global health expenditure. Chronic sovereign debt servicing starves public health ministries of funds to train doctors and nurses—who are simultaneously recruited away by wealthier health systems in an ongoing 'medical brain drain'—while rigid intellectual-property monopolies routinely price life-saving vaccines and biologics out of reach for developing nations.",
      "Equally troubling is the persistence of sharp health gradients within wealthy nations themselves. Even in prosperous democracies, residents of deindustrialized or marginalized neighborhoods frequently suffer life-expectancy gaps exceeding a decade compared to affluent suburbs just miles away—driven by what epidemiologists call the 'social determinants of health,' including substandard housing, air pollution, food deserts, and chronic economic stress.",
      "Investing in universal health coverage is sometimes dismissed by fiscal conservatives as an unaffordable budgetary drain. Empirical evidence demonstrates the exact opposite: healthy populations drive educational attainment, labor productivity, and social resilience, generating economic returns several times greater than initial public outlays.",
      "Ultimately, closing the global health divide requires treating healthcare not as a luxury commodity rationed by ability to pay, but as a foundational human right—supported by debt relief for public health systems, equitable technology transfers, and universal primary care accessible to every community on Earth."
