    "texte": [
      "Walk into any university counseling center, pediatric emergency department, or workplace human-resources office across the industrialized world today, and an unmistakable reality confronts you: societies are experiencing a profound, multi-generational mental health crisis. Reporting on public health and social policy as a medical columnist over the past fifteen years, I have watched anxiety disorders, clinical depression, burnout, and adolescent psychological distress surge to record levels—affecting nearly one billion people worldwide while remaining chronically underfunded and shrouded in lingering social stigma.",
       "For decades, healthcare systems operated under a Cartesian split that separated physical pathology from psychological suffering—investing heavily in cardiology or oncology while relegating psychiatric care and community therapy to under-resourced margins. In reality, mental and physical health are deeply intertwined: untreated psychological distress significantly elevates risks of cardiovascular disease, immune dysfunction, and premature mortality, while costing the global economy trillions of dollars annually in lost productivity.",
"Crucially, however, confronting this epidemic requires looking beyond purely neurochemical explanations or individual prescription pads to address the social ecology of psychological distress. Precarious employment, unaffordable housing, social isolation, algorithmic social media comparison, and acute climate anxiety among young people act as powerful structural drivers of emotional suffering.",
"Of course, psychiatric medications and evidence-based individual psychotherapy save countless lives and must be made universally accessible without financial barriers; yet medicating individuals while leaving toxic workplaces and fragmented communities untouched resembles treating respiratory illness without cleaning polluted air.",
"Transforming mental health into a genuine public health priority therefore demands a dual revolution: integrating free, community-based psychological care into primary healthcare systems while building humane workplaces, connected neighborhoods, and supportive schools that nurture emotional resilience before crisis strikes."
