"texte": [
"During an international bioethics roundtable in Geneva, reproductive endocrinologist Dr. Clara Sterling and moral philosopher Prof. Samuel Vance debated the ethical boundaries of modern assisted reproductive technologies (ART). 'Since the birth of the first child conceived via in vitro fertilization in 1978, reproductive medicine has brought profound joy to millions of infertile couples, single parents, and same-sex families,' Dr. Sterling observed. 'Today, pre-implantation genetic diagnosis (PGD) and egg cryopreservation further empower individuals to prevent devastating hereditary diseases and align parenthood with personal and professional autonomy.'",
"Prof. Vance acknowledged these compassionate medical achievements before raising complex moral questions: 'Helping families overcome infertility and avoid fatal genetic disorders is deeply humane, Clara, yet the rapid commercialization of reproductive medicine is transforming human procreation into an unregulated global market. When wealthy clients contract cross-border commercial surrogacy in low-income countries or use polygenic embryo screening to select embryos for non-medical traits like height or predicted intelligence, human children risk being treated as customized consumer products rather than unconditional gifts.'",
"'Commercial exploitation of vulnerable surrogates must certainly be prohibited through strict international legal safeguards,' Dr. Sterling agreed. 'However, drawing clear regulatory lines is complex: when parents undergo IVF and create multiple viable embryos, using genetic screening to select the healthiest embryo spares a future child from lifelong suffering. Surely compassionate prevention differs fundamentally from eugenic vanity?'",
"'The distinction between preventing severe pathology and selecting enhancement traits is morally vital, yet notoriously fragile in practice,' Prof. Vance replied. 'Without clear statutory boundaries, commercial fertility clinics face intense financial incentives to market ever-broader genetic selection menus—threatening to stigmatize people living with disabilities and deepen social inequality from the very moment of conception.'",
"By the close of their dialogue, both speakers affirmed that reproductive technologies must remain guided by human dignity rather than market logic. As Dr. Sterling concluded, 'Ethical reproductive medicine requires banning commercial exploitation, ensuring equitable public access to fertility care, and resisting the commodification of human life so every child is welcomed unconditionally.'"
