"texte": [
"Director-General, Ministers of Health, and distinguished delegates to the World Health Assembly, human memory is notoriously short when biological danger recedes. Throughout modern public health history, nations have swung between a destructive cycle of 'panic and neglect'—pouring emergency billions into hospitals and vaccine laboratories during the height of a terrifying outbreak, only to slash epidemiological surveillance budgets and dismantle stockpiles the moment headlines fade. Today, as rapid urbanization, global aviation, climate-driven vector migration, and habitat encroachment accelerate the spillover of novel pathogens, breaking this cycle of amnesia is an existential imperative.",
"Recent global health emergencies exposed two fatal vulnerabilities in our collective defenses. First, early warning systems remain dangerously fragmented: an outbreak emerging in an under-resourced rural region can spread across continents within thirty-six hours before local laboratories possess the genomic sequencing capacity to identify it. Second, global solidarity collapsed precisely when needed most: wealthy nations monopolized early vaccine and therapeutic supplies through bilateral advance-purchase contracts, leaving frontline health workers and vulnerable citizens in low-income countries unprotected for over a year.",
"In an interconnected biosphere, however, vaccine nationalism is not merely a moral failure—it is an epidemiological self-inflicted wound. As virologists repeatedly warned, no nation is truly safe from a rapidly mutating airborne pathogen until every nation possesses the tools to suppress transmission.",
"True epidemic preparedness therefore requires treating pandemic prevention as a permanent global security investment rather than an optional charitable expenditure. That means financing decentralized vaccine and diagnostic manufacturing hubs across Africa, Latin America, and Asia, establishing binding pathogen-sharing and benefit-sharing treaties, and strengthening resilient community primary healthcare networks.",
"Therefore, delegates, let us replace reactive panic with enduring global solidarity—building a world where scientific speed is matched by equitable access, and where no community is left defenseless against the next biological storm."
