"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Vance, across several high- and middle-income countries, private equity firms, for-profit hospital chains, and commercial insurance conglomerates have steadily expanded their footprint inside healthcare systems. Proponents argue that market competition boosts efficiency and innovation. Why do you contend that commercializing healthcare undermines both clinical quality and social equity?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Because healthcare violates virtually every textbook condition of a normal consumer market. When a patient suffers a heart attack or receives a cancer diagnosis, they are not an informed shopper comparing prices at leisure; they are vulnerable, frightened, and reliant on medical expertise. When hospitals and nursing homes are acquired by financial investors demanding rapid quarterly profit margins, cost-cutting inevitably targets nurse-to-patient ratios, emergency capacity, and unprofitable rural clinics—directly correlating with higher patient complication and mortality rates.",
"JOURNALIST: How does profit-driven healthcare affect financial security and preventive medicine for ordinary households?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Commercial insurance and fee-for-service billing create perverse incentives that reward expensive, high-tech procedures over patient prevention and primary care. Simultaneously, high deductibles and out-of-pocket charges deter low- and middle-income patients from seeking early diagnosis until illnesses become acute and life-threatening—driving millions of families worldwide into 'medical bankruptcy' every year simply for falling ill.",
"JOURNALIST: Yet public universal healthcare systems in many countries currently face long waiting lists, staff burnout, and strained budgets. Does private sector involvement offer any constructive role?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Public systems suffer underfunding largely because governments starve them of resources while subsidizing private alternatives. Targeted public-private partnerships can certainly assist in pharmaceutical manufacturing or specialized diagnostic equipment under strict price regulation; however, core clinical care and health insurance financing must remain publicly governed solidarity pools.",
"JOURNALIST: What core principle should guide healthcare reform so systems remain both financially sustainable and deeply humane?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Decommodifying essential care: financing healthcare through progressive taxation or universal social insurance so access is determined strictly by medical need rather than wallet size, while restoring clinical autonomy to doctors and nurses rather than corporate accountants. A civilized society never turns human suffering into a profit center."
