"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Vance, over the past half-century, industrialized agriculture and global food corporations have dramatically lowered calorie costs and prevented mass famines in many regions. Yet diet-related chronic diseases—obesity, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and cardiovascular illness—have surged worldwide. How has industrial food processing reshaped human nutrition?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: We have transitioned from eating whole, minimally processed foods to deriving over half of daily calories in many nations from 'ultra-processed foods' (UPFs). Formulated in corporate laboratories from refined starches, industrial seed oils, added sugars, emulsifiers, and synthetic flavorings, UPFs are engineered to hit a hyper-palatable 'bliss point' that overrides natural satiety signals—encouraging chronic overconsumption while stripping away dietary fiber, micronutrients, and beneficial gut-microbiome diversity.",
"JOURNALIST: Why do low-income households bear a disproportionate share of diet-related chronic disease even in wealthy countries?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Because our current food economy creates a perverse price distortion. Heavily subsidized industrial monocultures—corn, soy, wheat, and sugar—make calorie-dense, nutrient-poor ultra-processed snacks artificially cheap and ubiquitous, whereas fresh vegetables, fruits, legumes, and whole grains remain comparatively expensive and scarce in low-income 'food deserts.' Blaming disadvantaged families for poor dietary willpower ignores an economic environment systematically rigged against healthy eating.",
"JOURNALIST: Beyond direct human nutrition, how does industrial food production rebound upon public health through the environment?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Intensive chemical monocultures and concentrated animal feeding operations (CAFOs) degrade topsoil fertility, contaminate drinking aquifers with pesticide and nitrate runoff, accelerate antibiotic resistance, and generate nearly a third of global greenhouse gas emissions—undermining the very ecological stability upon which future food security depends.",
"JOURNALIST: What structural policy reforms can transform food systems so that nutritious, sustainable diets become accessible to everyone?\nPROFESSOR VANCE: Governments must shift agricultural subsidies away from industrial ultra-processed commodities toward agroecological fruit, vegetable, and pulse farming; enact clear front-of-package warning labels and taxes on sugary, ultra-processed products; restrict junk-food marketing aimed at children; and guarantee free, freshly cooked nutritious meals in all public schools. Healthy food is a fundamental public health right, not a luxury good."
