"texte": [
"During a public health ethics symposium in Edinburgh, classical liberal economist Dr. Julian Mercer and public health philosopher Prof. Clara Sterling debated where personal autonomy ends and legitimate state intervention begins in safeguarding population health. 'In a free society, adult citizens must retain sovereignty over their own bodies and lifestyle choices—whether regarding diet, alcohol, tobacco, or physical exercise,' Dr. Mercer argued. 'When governments impose sugar taxes, smoking bans, or mandatory health regulations, they risk sliding into intrusive paternalism that treats free adults like children while penalizing working-class consumers through regressive taxes.'",
"Prof. Sterling listened attentively before challenging the assumption that modern lifestyle choices occur in a neutral, unmanipulated vacuum: 'Personal agency certainly matters, Julian, yet framing health solely around individual willpower ignores how powerfully corporate marketing and urban environments shape behavior. When billion-dollar conglomerates engineer addictive nicotine or sugary products and saturate low-income neighborhoods with fast-food billboards while parks and safe sidewalks are lacking, apparent 'free choice' is heavily tilted toward illness.'",
