"texte": [
"Excellencies, Ministers of Trade, private-sector leaders, and distinguished delegates gathered for this Continental Economic Integration Summit, for more than six decades since the birth of the Pan-African movement, a glaring structural paradox has constrained Africa's economic emancipation: while Africa trades extensively with Europe, Asia, and North America—exporting raw commodities and importing finished manufactures—intra-African trade has historically accounted for barely fifteen percent of the continent's total commerce, compared to nearly sixty percent in Asia and almost seventy percent in Europe. Today, with the operationalization of the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA)—uniting 1.4 billion citizens across fifty-four nations into the world's largest free-trade area by number of countries—Africa possesses a historic instrument to dismantle inherited colonial trade fragmentation.",
"Why does deeper intra-African integration matter so profoundly for structural transformation? Unlike exports to distant global markets—which remain heavily dominated by unprocessed oil, minerals, and agricultural staples—trade among African neighbors consists predominantly of value-added manufactured goods, processed foods, pharmaceuticals, and regional services. Expanding a unified continental market enables African enterprises to achieve economies of scale, attract industrial investment, and shield domestic economies from external global supply shocks.",
"Yet signing a treaty in diplomatic halls does not automatically dissolve physical and administrative barriers on the ground. Tariffs are only part of the obstacle; persistent non-tariff barriers—such as dilapidated cross-border highways and railways, protracted customs delays at border posts, fragmented payment currencies, and divergent regulatory standards—still make shipping goods between neighboring African capitals costlier than importing them from another continent.",
"Furthermore, integration must be designed as an engine of inclusive, balanced industrialization rather than allowing a few larger economies to dominate smaller neighbors—requiring targeted regional development funds and cross-border agricultural and digital corridors.",
"Therefore, delegates, let us match diplomatic vision with relentless execution: harmonizing customs and digital payment systems, investing boldly in transcontinental transport and clean-energy corridors, and empowering small businesses, women traders, and young innovators to build a prosperous, self-reliant, and united African economy."
