    "Inside a sunlit agro-processing cooperative on the outskirts of Kigali, thirty-four-year-old entrepreneur Grace Uwimana inspected crates of sun-dried organic pineapple and mango slices ready for regional export. Five years earlier, Grace had started with two solar dehydrators and a modest microloan from a women's savings circle after commercial banks repeatedly rejected her business plan for lack of titled land collateral. Today, her enterprise employs forty-two full-time staff—predominantly rural women—and sources fruit from over three hundred smallholder farmers, illustrating a transformative reality: across Africa, where women boast the highest rate of entrepreneurial activity in the world, female-led enterprises are potent engines of inclusive growth and community resilience.",
