    "texte": [
      "Every transformative human invention enters the world wrapped in utopian promise, solving an immediate, tangible problem while quietly planting the seeds of unforeseen systemic disruption. When Thomas Midgley Jr. synthesized chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) and leaded gasoline in the 1920s, engineers hailed them as miraculous breakthroughs in refrigeration safety and engine efficiency—entirely unaware that decades later those compounds would erode Earth's stratospheric ozone layer and inflict widespread neurodevelopmental harm. Similarly, the internal combustion engine, synthetic plastics, antibiotics, and global social networks each liberated humanity from ancient constraints while generating cascading ecological, biological, and psychological side effects that their creators never imagined.",
      "Philosophers of technology describe this recurring pattern through the 'Collingridge dilemma': during the early stages of an innovation's development, its long-term societal and environmental impacts cannot yet be reliably predicted; yet by the time those unintended consequences become undeniable, the technology has become so deeply embedded in economic infrastructure and daily habits that regulating or replacing it proves extraordinarily costly.",
