"texte": [
"When environmental thinkers warn about resource depletion, public attention naturally turns to tangible physical reserves—dwindling freshwater aquifers, tropical forests, rare-earth minerals, and fertile topsoil. Yet writing on cultural and economic life as a social columnist over the past fifteen years, I have become convinced that late-stage consumer capitalism is simultaneously exhausting an equally vital, invisible reservoir: the intangible human and civic resources of sustained attention, unhurried time, quiet solitude, neighborly trust, and non-commercial public space.",
"Modern consumer culture operates on a perpetual engine of manufactured dissatisfaction. Through ubiquitous targeted advertising, influencer lifestyle marketing, and planned product obsolescence, citizens are conditioned to equate identity, emotional solace, and social belonging with endless material acquisition. As economist Fred Hirsch insightfully observed in 'Social Limits to Growth,' once basic material comforts are satisfied, consumer competition shifts toward 'positional goods'—status symbols whose value depends on exclusivity—locking households into an exhausting cycle of overwork and debt merely to keep pace.",
"This relentless commercialization steadily colonizes spheres of human life that once stood outside market exchange. Public squares become outdoor shopping malls; quiet evening hours are monetized by attention-harvesting streaming algorithms; and spontaneous community hospitality is increasingly replaced by paid convenience services.",
"Of course, rising material productivity has liberated millions from arduous physical toil and expanded access to essential household comforts; the critique is aimed not at material sufficiency, but at hyper-consumerism that cannibalizes non-material well-being.",
"Reclaiming human flourishing in affluent societies therefore requires protecting 'intangible commons': safeguarding commercial-free public parks and libraries, establishing legal rights to disconnect from work, curbing intrusive advertising in public spaces, and rediscovering that the richest dimensions of a good life—friendship, contemplation, civic solidarity, and beauty—cannot be bought."
],
