"texte": [
"JOURNALIST: Professor Lindholm, with the global human population surpassing eight billion and projected to peak near ten billion later this century, public debate frequently revives Thomas Malthus's eighteenth-century warning that human numbers will inevitably outstrip Earth's ecological carrying capacity. How does contemporary demography view the relationship between population size and planetary limits?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: Modern demography reveals a far more nuanced picture than simplistic Malthusian alarmism. First, global population growth has already slowed dramatically—falling from over two percent annually in the 1960s to under one percent today—and is projected to stabilize and gradually decline before the end of the century as girls' education, healthcare, and urbanization advance worldwide. Second, attributing ecological crisis simply to raw human headcount obscures a massive global inequality: the richest ten percent of the world's population generates nearly half of all global carbon emissions, whereas the poorest half contributes barely ten percent.",
"JOURNALIST: Why is framing environmental degradation solely as an 'overpopulation problem' in developing countries both scientifically inaccurate and ethically dangerous?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: Because it scapegoats high-fertility, low-consuming populations in the Global South while letting high-consuming, low-fertility elites in wealthy nations off the hook. A single child born into an affluent, high-carbon consumer lifestyle commands an ecological footprint dozens of times larger than a child born in a low-income rural community. Historically, panic over 'overpopulation' has also been used to justify coercive population-control policies that violated women's bodily autonomy.",
"JOURNALIST: At the same time, many countries across Europe, East Asia, and Latin America now face the opposite demographic shift: rapid population aging and shrinking workforces. How should societies navigate both ecological limits and demographic transition?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: By recognizing that human rights and ecological sustainability reinforce one another. Whenever governments invest in universal girls' secondary education, voluntary family planning, maternal healthcare, and social security pensions, birth rates decline naturally and peacefully without coercion—while empowering women and stabilizing population within planetary boundaries.",
"JOURNALIST: Ultimately, what matters most for sustaining ten billion human beings with dignity on a finite planet?\nPROFESSOR LINDQVIST: Shifting from an obsession with population numbers to transforming how we produce, consume, and share resources: transitioning to clean energy, adopting circular economies, curbing extreme overconsumption among the affluent, and guaranteeing dignified human development for every person on Earth."
